ned in at least three other experiments. <sup>&#x0002a;</sup><italic>P</italic>&#x02a7d;0.01. <sup>&#x0002a;&#x0002a;</sup><italic>P</italic>&#x02a7d;0.001, <sup>&#x0002a;&#x0002a;&#x0002a;</sup><italic>P</italic>&#x02a7d;0.0001. &#x025cb;, proliferative effect of 10&#x00025; FCS. The statistical test used is an unpaired <italic>t</italic>-test.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="gif" xlink:href="92-6602535f1"/></fig><fig id="fig2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Expression of CCK2 receptor and HGF receptor (c-Met) in BON cells. RT&#x02013;PCR analysis of total RNA was performed with (&#x0002b;) or without (&#x02212;) RT to rule out contamination of genomic DNA. PCR products were visualised in ethidium bromide-stained 1.2&#x00025; agarose gels, and showed the expected sizes of 398&#x02009;bp (CCK2 receptor) and 395&#x02009;bp (c-Met), respectively. LoVo cells were used as a positive control for CCK2 receptor (<xref ref-type="other" rid="bib57">Watson <italic>et al</italic>, 1998</xref>) and c-Met (<xref ref-type="other" rid="bib35">Nabeshima <italic>et al</italic>, 1998</xref>). The results shown are representative of three independent experiments. M: 100&#x02009;bp DNA ladder molecular weight marker.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="jpeg" xlink:href="92-6602535f2"/></fig><table-wrap id="tbl1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p content-type="table-title">Primer sequences of selected genes for confirmation studies</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups" border="1" width="85%"><colgroup><col align="left"/><col align="left"/><col align="char" char="."/></colgroup><thead valign="bottom"><tr><th align="left" valign="top" charoff="50"><bold>Gene</bold></th><th align="left" valign="top" charoff="50">&#x000a0;</th><th align="char" valign="top" char="." charoff="50"><bold>PCR products (bp)</bold></th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td align="left" vali